Article 9
Child victims of violence in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 8 also obtain special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
Protection of Women and Children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of women and children;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. respect for the child's opinion;
e. respect for the rights of victims;
f. legal certainty; and
g. local wisdom.

Article 11
(1) Regional Governments have the obligation and responsibility to implement protection efforts for women and children victims of violence through:
a. implementing protection policies for women and children victims of violence applied by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and protection activities for women and children victims of violence;
c. establishment of P2TP2A;
d. ensuring the implementation of service facilitation for victims;
e. pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and
f. pursuing the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
(2) Regional Governments in carrying out obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, shall establish action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall serve as the basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
(4) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(5) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be further regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

